Kernel 19 — Bifrost: External Capability Bridge
Mission

Build Bifrost, Pantheon’s governed bridge between the core organism and software, services, protocols, plugins, MCP servers, APIs, models, and other external capabilities.

Pantheon should be able to gain new capabilities without teaching every daemon how every outside system works and without silently granting outside software authority over Pantheon.

Bifrost connects worlds. It does not rule either one.

This kernel should also establish the first practical Bifrost capability: replacing Hermes/Terra’s existing email-triage machinery with a bounded, deterministic mail-intake path suitable for Pantheon.

1. Architectural Contract

Bifrost is infrastructure, not a member of the main Pantheon roster.

It lives outside the primary daemon code wherever practical and provides a governed boundary through which approved external capabilities may be:

discovered
described
installed
configured
enabled/disabled
invoked
updated
inspected
revoked
audited

Design for multiple integration forms. Do not assume everything is an MCP.

Anticipated capability types include at minimum:

MCP servers
command-line programs
local applications
local HTTP services
remote APIs
plugins/extensions
model providers
filesystem-backed tools
imported/open-source software
future protocols not yet selected

Prefer a common capability abstraction where one can honestly exist, but do not mutilate fundamentally different systems to force them into one interface.

2. Authority Boundary

Installing software and authorizing its use are deliberate human decisions.

Grant must approve:

download
installation
registration with Bifrost
initial authorization/use

These are separate events and should remain distinguishable in receipts/audit history even when the UI eventually presents them as a convenient sequence.

Pantheon must not discover an interesting tool on the Internet and silently install it.

Pantheon must not install something and interpret installation as permission to use it.

Pantheon must not use an installed but unapproved capability merely because it is technically available.

Certain capabilities may later receive standing permission for automatic updates. That permission must be explicit, scoped, revocable, and visible.

Automatic-update permissions should surface through the same reminder/notification system used for other persistent permissions so that old grants do not disappear into forgotten configuration.

Default:

No download, installation, activation, or first use without approval.

3. Capability Registry

Create a Bifrost registry capable of answering, at minimum:

What is this capability?
Where did it come from?
What version is installed?
How is it invoked?
What does it claim it can do?
What resources can it access?
Does it require network access?
Does it send data off-machine?
What authentication/secrets does it require?
What Pantheon components may invoke it?
What operations are authorized?
What operations remain prohibited?
Was its download approved?
Was installation approved?
Was use approved?
Are automatic updates permitted?
When was it last invoked?
What changed since approval?
How can it be disabled?
How can it be removed?

Where the underlying plugin/protocol exposes useful metadata, ingest it rather than duplicating it manually.

Where it does not, Bifrost must maintain the missing governance metadata itself.

Do not pretend an external tool is safer or more understandable than the evidence supports.

4. Capability Descriptions

A capability should expose a human-readable description in addition to machine metadata.

Eventually Grant should be able to ask questions such as:

What can this thing actually do?

Why does Pantheon want it?

What can it see?

What can it change?

What leaves my computer?

Who is currently allowed to use it?

What would break if I turn it off?

The registry should support those answers without requiring Grant to understand MCP schemas, manifests, OAuth scopes, CLI flags, or implementation details.

Preserve the underlying technical information for inspection.

5. Prometheus Governance

Bifrost does not bypass Prometheus.

External capabilities remain subject to Pantheon’s existing authority and safety architecture.

Prometheus must be capable of:

seeing registered capabilities
seeing enabled capabilities
seeing current/recent invocations
preventing invocation
revoking authorization
constraining network/tool chains where applicable
participating in the emergency pause

pantheon prometheus protocol --pause

must prevent Pantheon from initiating new Bifrost activity.

Do not create a second competing permission/control plane inside Bifrost.

Bifrost records and enforces capability-specific grants; Prometheus remains the overarching control plane.

6. Mercury and Tool Execution

Do not keep Terra riding Hermes merely because Hermes currently knows how to operate external tools.

Mercury is sufficiently deterministic to carry authorized work to deterministic external capabilities.

The intended pattern is:

Future/order source → lawful order → Mercury → Bifrost → approved capability

where that architecture applies.

Mercury does not gain discretion merely because Bifrost gives it more destinations.

Bifrost does not decide that work should occur.

It provides the governed crossing.

If an external capability requires reasoning before safe invocation, that reasoning belongs to the appropriate Pantheon intelligence before a lawful order reaches the execution path.

7. Apollo and Aphrodite Visibility

Apollo must be able to inspect Bifrost’s capability registry and understand what Pantheon can and cannot currently do.

Future Aphrodite work must be able to inspect:

installed capabilities
capability descriptions
versions
permissions
invocation history
failures
relevant system readings

Aphrodite may eventually use those readings while working inside her sandbox and deliver findings through Pantheon to Apollo.

Apollo or another appropriately authorized component may use Internet research to investigate an external capability and explain it.

Researching software does not authorize downloading, installing, or using it.

This kernel should establish the information structures needed for that future work without implementing Project Fame or Project Purity.

8. First Bifrost Crossing: Email

Use the existing personal Gmail workflow as Bifrost’s first substantial real-world integration.

The current Hermes implementation has useful work worth preserving but should not remain the permanent architecture.

Current flow approximately includes:

Himalaya → bounded manifest → Terra/GPT reasoning → mailbox operations

Replace the routine ingestion portion with a deterministic Pantheon path.

Target:

approved mail interface → Bifrost → deterministic mail-intake daemon → Pantheon Intake

Interpretation happens later:

Intake → Present / appropriate reasoning path → Apollo → downstream Pantheon work

The mail-intake daemon is not Apollo.

It does not summarize Grant’s life.

It does not decide what deserves his attention.

It gets mail safely across the bridge.

9. Preserve Existing Mail Safety Work

Use the existing Hermes scripts as implementation evidence, not permanent dependencies:

/home/brick/.hermes/scripts/new_mail_triage_manifest.py
/home/brick/.hermes/scripts/overnight_backlog_batch.py

The existing system already demonstrates useful patterns:

bounded manifests
precise baseline/checkpoint state
processed-ID deduplication
fixed historical batches
safe handling of changing mailbox IDs
blocking when exact mapping cannot be established
no uncontrolled historical-mail discovery during bounded processing

Preserve or improve these properties.

Do not blindly transplant Hermes code into Pantheon.

10. Deterministic Mail Intake

The new mail daemon should eventually be capable of checking approved accounts on a schedule, initially hourly.

For each newly eligible message, create a clean Intake representation containing appropriate available metadata such as:

stable source identifier
message identifier
account
received timestamp
sender
recipients
subject
normalized body/content
attachment metadata
source/provenance
ingestion timestamp
ingestion status
warnings/block reasons

Preserve the original email in the mail provider.

Do not make Pantheon’s Intake copy the authoritative mailbox.

A failed Intake operation must not cause loss of the original message.

11. Attachments

Attachments cross a more restrictive bridge than ordinary message text.

Implement a small explicit allowlist of attachment types that Pantheon can safely ingest.

Do not construct a giant blacklist and assume everything omitted is safe.

Files outside the allowlist are blocked from automatic ingestion.

This explicitly includes formats that require proprietary/special readers or present opaque/encrypted content that Pantheon cannot safely inspect.

HTML attachments should not be treated as ordinary safe documents.

For blocked attachments:

preserve the fact that the attachment exists
preserve safe metadata where available
do not execute/open/render it merely to determine its contents
mark the Intake record with the reason it was blocked
surface an appropriate warning through Pantheon’s existing notification/escalation architecture when warranted

The email itself should still be ingestible when an attachment is blocked.

12. Mailbox Mutation

The deterministic daemon may perform only explicitly authorized deterministic mailbox operations.

Initial safe scope may include:

applying known labels/tags
marking messages read
temporarily marking important where a deterministic rule explicitly requires it
moving/categorizing mail according to established deterministic rules

It must not autonomously:

send
reply
forward
delete
archive
schedule sending
alter account configuration

Archive remains approval-gated.

Draft composition belongs to a reasoning path, not this daemon.

Newsletter unsubscribe behavior should not be migrated into the first deterministic mail daemon. It requires behavioral interpretation/state and can be redesigned separately.

When classification cannot be established deterministically, preserve the message and surface uncertainty rather than guessing.

13. Email Management Policy

Treat the existing Obsidian procedure as source policy:

/home/brick/Documents/Obsidian Vault/30 Procedures/Email Management.md

Do not encode the entire document into the deterministic mail daemon.

Separate mechanical policy from judgment.

Examples of reasoning work that should remain outside the daemon include:

relationship inference
importance
urgency
contextual priority points
whether something deserves Grant’s attention
semantic suspiciousness
whether a message deserves a reply
drafting
summarization
interpreting what a deadline means
learning what Grant finds worthy
morning-brief composition
reflection prompts
historical-memory selection

The deterministic daemon may attach facts that make those judgments easier downstream.

14. Loved-One Safety

Preserve the existing hard requirement:

Pantheon must never autonomously respond to Grant’s loved ones or cause their messages to be lost, deleted, hidden, or otherwise unavailable for him to enjoy.

Do not rely solely on probabilistic relationship inference to enforce destructive-action safety.

Where Pantheon cannot establish that a destructive operation is safe, it must not perform it.

15. Email → Vault

Bifrost should establish a path by which selected email can eventually become Vault material.

Grant specifically wants his own authored email available as evidence of his writing voice.

Do not dump the entire mailbox into the Vault.

Design for selection such as Sent-mail authored by Grant, with deterministic filtering where reliable.

Preserve:

provenance
timestamps
useful conversation context where appropriate
source identity
enough metadata to distinguish Grant-authored language from quoted/replied material

Do not implement a naïve “50,000 emails → Vault” importer.

This capability may use an MCP if MCP is the best interface, but MCP is an implementation option, not the architectural requirement.

16. Hermes Migration Boundary

Do not destroy Hermes during this kernel.

We need a controlled migration.

Existing Hermes jobs include:

hourly new-mail-triage
overnight overnight-backlog-triage
the now-unwanted Hermes-authored Situation Brief and its verification machinery

The new Pantheon email crossing must be proven before the corresponding Hermes mail jobs are removed.

Avoid two systems mutating the same messages simultaneously.

Provide an explicit migration/cutover procedure.

Retain enough rollback information to restore the previous path temporarily if the new one fails.

Hermes should cease being necessary for routine email ingestion once Bifrost’s replacement is proven.

17. Scheduling

Pantheon does not yet have its final calendar/email scheduling architecture.

Do not solve the entire scheduler in Kernel 19.

Implement only enough deterministic scheduling to prove the email intake path, or expose the operation so an existing lawful scheduler can trigger it.

The architectural capability should not depend on Hermes cron permanently.

Hourly mail retrieval is the intended initial cadence.

18. Notifications and Persistent Permissions

Bifrost should use Pantheon’s existing notification/escalation concepts rather than inventing an independent alert system.

Examples worth surfacing include:

blocked attachment requiring attention
capability authorization failure
authentication expiry
external capability unexpectedly unavailable
update awaiting approval
changed permissions/scopes
repeated invocation failure

Persistent automatic permissions, particularly automatic updates, should be periodically resurfaced so Grant remembers what Pantheon is allowed to do.

Do not nag for ordinary healthy operation.

19. Secrets

Bifrost must not become a plaintext credential warehouse.

Reuse secure existing credential mechanisms where appropriate.

Capability metadata may state that authentication exists without exposing the secret.

Logs, receipts, Apollo reports, error messages, and Vault records must not casually reproduce:

passwords
API keys
OAuth tokens
session secrets
authentication headers

A capability losing authentication should fail closed and report the problem.

20. Failure Semantics

External systems are unreliable.

Bifrost must distinguish at least:

unavailable
unauthorized
approval required
authentication expired
unsupported operation
malformed response
blocked by policy
blocked by Prometheus
timeout
external failure
local adapter failure

Do not turn every failure into generic "tool failed".

A failed external operation must not be represented to the rest of Pantheon as successful.

Where side effects may have partially occurred, record uncertainty explicitly.

21. Audit Receipts

Meaningful Bifrost activity should produce useful receipts.

At minimum capture:

capability
invocation time
requesting Pantheon component
governing authorization/order where applicable
operation
result
whether external network activity occurred
relevant non-secret error state

Keep receipts useful enough for Prometheus, Apollo, future Aphrodite analysis, debugging, and human inspection without creating an indiscriminate surveillance dump.

22. CLI

Add a small, coherent CLI surface.

Exact syntax may follow existing Pantheon conventions, but functionality should approximately support:

pantheon bifrost status
pantheon bifrost list
pantheon bifrost show <capability>
pantheon bifrost approvals
pantheon bifrost enable <capability>
pantheon bifrost disable <capability>

Operations requiring Grant’s authorization must actually require authorization.

Do not implement fake confirmation by merely accepting --yes from an autonomous caller.

Also expose enough email-specific functionality to inspect the first crossing, for example:

pantheon bifrost mail status
pantheon bifrost mail intake

Prefer a small discoverable command tree over dozens of commands.

23. Extensibility

Adding the second and third external capabilities should be substantially easier than adding the first.

A new adapter should not require rewriting Prometheus, Mercury, Apollo, or the Intake architecture.

Favor:

explicit adapter contracts
capability manifests
shared authorization structures
shared receipts
shared failure semantics
narrow interfaces

Avoid:

giant switch statements keyed to product names
arbitrary shell execution as the universal plugin API
plugins importing Pantheon internals
external tools writing directly into arbitrary Pantheon state
tool-specific permission logic scattered throughout the organism
24. Do Not Build Yet

Kernel 19 should establish foundations, not consume every future project.

Do not build:

Project Fame
Project Purity
Aphrodite herself
the full GUI
live-transcription interpretation
automatic voice-command execution
the final morning briefing
the final Pantheon scheduler
a plugin marketplace
autonomous Internet software acquisition
automatic code modification by plugins
a universal compatibility layer for every conceivable protocol

Kernel 20 is expected to address the GUI. Do not let Bifrost become Kernel 20 accidentally.

25. Verification

Before declaring PASS, prove at minimum:

Registry
A capability can be registered.
Its source/type/version are inspectable.
Its permissions are inspectable.
It can be disabled.
Disabled means invocation actually fails closed.
Approval

Prove that an unapproved capability cannot be:

downloaded by Pantheon
installed by Pantheon
invoked by Pantheon

Prove separately that approval state is recorded.

Prometheus

With Pantheon paused:

new Bifrost invocations do not proceed.

Resume must restore normal governed operation without corrupting state.

Email

Using safe real or controlled email:

Retrieve new mail through the new crossing.
Produce a normalized Intake artifact.
Preserve provenance.
Do not alter the source message beyond explicitly authorized behavior.
Run again and prove deduplication.
Ingest an allowed attachment.
Encounter a blocked attachment and prove:
email ingestion continues;
attachment is not opened;
warning metadata survives.
Simulate connector failure and prove no message is lost.
Demonstrate bounded historical processing without uncontrolled mailbox discovery.
Hermes coexistence

Prove the migration procedure prevents Hermes and Pantheon from concurrently performing conflicting mail mutations.

Secrets

Inspect generated logs/receipts and demonstrate that credentials are not exposed.

Failure reporting

Demonstrate at least:

authorization-required
unavailable
authentication failure
policy block
Prometheus block

as distinguishable outcomes.

